# Why is the Frequency Solution Better?

Your solution is elegant and takes advantage of the fact that the input is already a palindrome. However, it still performs a comparison-based sort on half of the string, costing **O(n log n)** time.

The frequency-counting solution avoids sorting entirely:

* Counting characters: **O(n)**
* Processing 26 letters: **O(1)**
* Building the palindrome: **O(n)**

So its overall complexity is **O(n)**, making it asymptotically faster while using the same amount of extra space. It is the optimal approach for this problem because the alphabet size is fixed (26 lowercase English letters).
